      "technical_explanation": "The obfuscated-code signal does not correspond to any obfuscation in the supplied source. The flagged script is plain, readable JavaScript with clear function names, explanatory comments, and Korean-language UI strings that match the project's stated purpose of reorganizing the SillyTavern extensions panel into a folder-based, mobile-friendly list. The visible code contains no obfuscation indicators: no dynamic code evaluation, no encoded or hex-literal string payloads, no packed or self-decoding segments, and no concealed network requests. Its data flows are local and purpose-matched: it reads and writes its own settings through the SillyTavern extension-settings API, rearranges existing extensions-panel DOM into its own UI containers, and offers a user-initiated clipboard copy of a layout diagnostic report for bug-reporting. The scanner flagged with low scanner confidence and did not retain the matched literals; the prevalence of non-ASCII content (Korean text, emoji, and arrow glyphs used for titles and icons) most plausibly tripped the heuristic. No attacker-controlled path, external destination, or hidden behavior is present in the supplied evidence.",
      "layman_explanation": "An automated scanner suggested this code might be disguised or 'obfuscated' to hide what it does. Reading the actual code, it is ordinary and easy to follow: it saves the extension's own folder, sorting, and theme choices; reorganizes the on-screen extension list; and provides a copy button for a diagnostic layout report. The warning was almost certainly a false alarm caused by the Korean text and special symbols in the code confusing the scanner, rather than by anything actually hidden in it.",
